values (
  $kc$alp-cbat-vision-certificate-annexure-via$kc$,
  $kc$ALP CBAT Vision Certificate (Annexure VI-A): the format, how to get it made, and the mistakes that stop you at the gate$kc$,
  $kc$No original vision certificate in the Annexure VI-A format, no entry to the CBAT: that is the RRB's notice of 8 October 2026 for CEN 01/2025. Here is the format in Hindi and English, what every box means, who may sign it, and a step-by-step guide to getting it made without a mistake.$kc$,
  $kc$> **RRB notice of 8 October 2026, CEN 01/2025 (ALP):** candidates must produce the vision certificate **in original**, in the prescribed format (Annexure VI-A), at the time of entry for the Computer Based Aptitude Test. A candidate without it is not allowed to attend the exam.

The psycho test has a gate before the screen. On the day of the CBAT, before you reach the computer, you hand over a vision certificate: a one-page form, Annexure VI-A of CEN 01/2025, filled and signed by an eye specialist, with your photograph on it. The RRB's notice of 8 October 2026 says in plain words that without this certificate, in original and in this format, you are not allowed into the exam. Months of preparation for the five tests can end at the gate over a missing seal.

This article gives the format in Hindi and English, exactly as the RRB published it, explains every box, says who may sign it and what the seal must show, and walks through getting it made, step by step, so that nothing is left for the gate to find. The five tests themselves are in the [complete guide to the RRB ALP psycho test](/rrb-alp-psycho-test); the day of the exam is in [exam day in the ALP psycho test](/blog/alp-psycho-test-exam-day-tips).

## Download the format (Hindi and English)

:::box
# Vision Certificate: Download Now
The RRB notice of 8 October 2026 for CEN 01/2025 (ALP), with Annexure VI-A in English and in Hindi, as a PDF of 4 pages: the notice on pages 1 and 2, the Hindi format on page 3, the English format on page 4. Opens on the RRB website, rrb.indianrailways.gov.in.
[⬇ Download the vision certificate format (PDF)](https://rrb.indianrailways.gov.in/-/image/1791524982443Notice_Vision_certificate_format_CBAT_CEN_01-2025(ALP).pdf/examsDocuments)
:::

Print the page you will use on A4 paper, full size, with nothing cut off at the edges. The notice carries the format in both languages; the English one (page 4) is the Annexure as it stands in the CEN, so print that one unless your eye specialist prefers to fill the Hindi one (page 3). Print two copies, so that a slip of the pen does not cost you a second visit.

## What the RRB notice says, point by point

1. The tentative schedule of the CBAT was published on the official websites of the RRBs on 1 October 2026.
2. Candidates must produce the vision certificate in original, in the prescribed format (Annexure VI-A of CEN 01/2025), at the time of entry for the CBAT. A candidate who does not is not allowed to attend the exam.
3. Some words overlapped in the format printed in the CEN, so the RRB has reproduced the same format with clarity, and instructs candidates once again to produce this format for the CBAT.
4. There is no change in the prescribed format. It is the Annexure VI-A originally published in CEN 01/2025 (ALP).
5. The seal of the eye specialist must clearly indicate their ophthalmology-related education and their position in that hospital as eye specialist.

Point 5 is the one that catches candidates. A signature and a round stamp with a name on it are not enough. The seal has to say what the doctor studied in ophthalmology and what post they hold at that hospital.

## The format: Annexure VI-A

The English page, as the RRB published it:

![Annexure VI-A, the vision certificate format for the RRB ALP CBAT, CEN 01/2025, in English](/blog/alp-vision-certificate-annexure-via-english.png)

The Hindi page of the same notice:

![Annexure VI-A, the vision certificate format for the RRB ALP CBAT, CEN 01/2025, in Hindi](/blog/alp-vision-certificate-annexure-via-hindi.png)

## What each part of the certificate means

- **The heading.** "Proforma for medical certificate to be obtained from an eye specialist by candidates applying for the post of Assistant Loco Pilot against CEN No. 01/2025." This heading is how the gate knows it is the right form. A certificate on a hospital's own letterhead, however detailed, is not this format.
- **Your name.** "I have examined Shri / Smt / Kum ......" The eye specialist writes your name exactly as it is in your online application.
- **The photo box.** A colour passport-size photograph, 3.5 cm × 4.5 cm, the same photograph you used for the online application, pasted in the box and attested by the eye specialist. Under it, your own signature.
- **The table, four columns.** For each, a required standard is printed and the specialist writes the actual observation or value for your eyes:
- **Distant vision:** required 6/6, 6/6 without glasses, with the fogging test, which must not accept +2D. In the fogging test the specialist puts a +2D lens before the eye; a normal eye blurs with it, while an eye with hidden long-sightedness still reads the chart. "Must not accept +2D" means your eye must not read 6/6 through that lens.
- **Near vision:** required Sn. 0.6, 0.6 without glasses. Sn. 0.6 is the reading line on the Snellen near-vision chart, each eye on its own, with no glasses.
- **Colour vision:** required Normal. Usually the Ishihara colour plates.
- **Binocular vision, field of vision and night vision:** required Normal.
- **The verdict line.** "Shri / Smt / Kum ...... fully conforms / does not conform to the above vision standards." The specialist strikes out one of the two. A line with neither struck out is an unfilled certificate.
- **The surgery line.** "It is also certified that he/she has not undergone LASIK surgery or any surgery to correct refractive error." It stays as printed and the specialist's signature covers it.
- **The specialist's details.** Name of the eye specialist, registration number of the eye specialist, place, date, and the signature and seal. Every one of these is a box to be filled; a blank registration number is the commonest gap.

## Who may sign it: the eye specialist, the seal and the registration number

- **An eye specialist**, which means an ophthalmologist: a doctor with a postgraduate qualification in ophthalmology (MS or MD in Ophthalmology, DNB in Ophthalmology, or a Diploma in Ophthalmology) working as an eye specialist at a hospital.
- **The seal** must show that qualification and that position. A seal that reads, for example, "Dr. (name), MS (Ophthalmology), Eye Specialist / Senior Consultant, (hospital name)" meets point 5 of the notice. A seal with only a name, or a hospital's general stamp, does not.
- **The registration number** of the specialist, with the state medical council or the National Medical Commission, goes in its box on the certificate.
- **Signature and seal both**, on the certificate, and the specialist's attestation on the photograph.
- **Not enough:** an optometrist, an optician or a spectacle shop; a general physician or any doctor whose seal shows no ophthalmology qualification; a certificate signed by the hospital's administration instead of the examining specialist.

Government or private? The notice names no kind of hospital; it names the doctor. The surest places are the eye department of a government or district hospital, a medical college hospital, or a railway hospital, because their specialists' seals carry the designation as a matter of routine. A private eye hospital's ophthalmologist is acceptable when the seal carries the qualification and the position. Pindel Sir's rule for his batches: read the seal before you leave the hospital, with the notice's point 5 in your hand.

## Step by step: getting the certificate made

1. **Print the format.** Two copies of the page you will use, on A4, full size, from the Download Now button above.
2. **Collect what you will carry.** Two colour passport photographs, 3.5 cm × 4.5 cm, identical to the one in your online application; a printout of your application or registration details, so that the name is spelt the same; a photo identity card; and a printout of the RRB notice, to show the specialist what the seal must say.
3. **Choose the hospital and the specialist.** The eye department of a government or district hospital, a medical college, a railway hospital, or a private eye hospital whose ophthalmologist's seal shows qualification and position. Tell them at the counter that you need the Railway ALP vision certificate in the Annexure VI-A format.
4. **Give the examination without glasses or contact lenses.** The standard is unaided vision. The specialist reads your distant vision in each eye, does the fogging test with the +2D lens, reads your near vision in each eye, tests colour vision with the plates, and checks binocular vision, field of vision and night vision.
5. **See every "actual observation" box filled.** All four columns, nothing blank, in the specialist's hand.
6. **See the verdict line struck.** Either "fully conforms" or "does not conform" crossed out, leaving one.
7. **Leave the surgery line as printed.** Do not strike it, do not let anyone strike it.
8. **Name, registration number, place, date, signature, seal.** All six, and the seal with the qualification and the position on it.
9. **The photograph.** Pasted in the box, attested by the specialist with a signature or stamp that runs partly onto the photograph, and your signature under it.
10. **Before you leave the hospital,** read the whole page against the list below. Then take two photocopies and a photo on your phone, put the original in a plastic sleeve, and keep it where the admit card will be.

## When to get it made

The schedule came on 1 October 2026 and the notice on the certificate on 8 October 2026, so the window is now. The notice gives no validity period for the certificate, so a certificate made today serves for the CBAT; do not wait for the admit card to be released before you go to the hospital. When your CBAT call letter arrives, read it for anything more it asks you to bring, and carry that too.

## Mistakes that get candidates turned away or into trouble

- A photocopy, a scan on the phone, or a laminated copy instead of the original.
- A certificate in a different format: an older print with the overlapping words, a hospital letterhead, a general fitness certificate, or a certificate from an earlier stage of the recruitment.
- A seal that shows the doctor's name but not the ophthalmology qualification and the position at the hospital.
- A certificate from an optometrist, an optician or a general physician.
- A blank "actual observation" box in any of the four columns.
- The verdict line with neither "fully conforms" nor "does not conform" struck out.
- No registration number, no date, or no place.
- No photograph, a photograph different from the online application, or a photograph not attested by the specialist.
- Your signature missing under the photograph.
- Your name spelt differently from the application.
- The surgery line struck out or altered.

## If your eyes do not meet the standard

The standard on the certificate is the medical standard of the Assistant Loco Pilot post, and the railway tests the same eyes again at its own medical examination after selection. A certificate that says "does not conform" cannot be mended by another doctor's pen, and a candidate who has had LASIK or any surgery to correct refractive error cannot be certified on this form. If you wear glasses for distance, have your unaided vision read before you worry; many candidates who wear glasses for comfort still read 6/6 without them. Talk to the eye specialist plainly. It is better to know now than at the gate.

## The CBAT-day checklist

- The vision certificate in original, in the Annexure VI-A format ([download the format](https://rrb.indianrailways.gov.in/-/image/1791524982443Notice_Vision_certificate_format_CBAT_CEN_01-2025(ALP).pdf/examsDocuments)), with the attested photograph, the seal and the registration number.
- Your CBAT call letter or admit card, and the identity proof it names.
- Anything more the call letter asks for.
- A night's sleep and an early start, as RDSO advises in [exam day in the ALP psycho test](/blog/alp-psycho-test-exam-day-tips).

The certificate gets you to the screen. What happens on the screen is the five tests, and a T-Score of 42 or more in each. [Create a free account](/signup?next=/mocks) and sit a free Full Mock in the hall's order, with the hall's timing and a T-Score in every test; the [list of all 20 kinds of question](/psycho-test) shows what each test can ask.

## हिंदी में: ALP CBAT का विज़न सर्टिफिकेट क्या है और कैसे बनवाएँ

RRB की 8 अक्टूबर 2026 की सूचना (CEN 01/2025, ALP) के अनुसार CBAT में प्रवेश के समय निर्धारित फॉर्मेट (अनुलग्नक VI-A) में **मूल** विज़न सर्टिफिकेट दिखाना अनिवार्य है; इसके बिना परीक्षा में बैठने की अनुमति नहीं मिलेगी। फॉर्मेट में कोई बदलाव नहीं है; सूचना में वही फॉर्मेट हिंदी और अंग्रेज़ी में साफ़ छपा है, और नीचे दिए Download Now बटन से डाउनलोड किया जा सकता है। बनवाने के कदम:

1. फॉर्मेट A4 पर पूरा प्रिंट करें, दो प्रतियाँ।
2. साथ ले जाएँ: 3.5 × 4.5 सेमी के दो रंगीन पासपोर्ट फोटो (वही जो ऑनलाइन आवेदन में लगाया था), आवेदन का प्रिंट, पहचान पत्र और RRB की सूचना का प्रिंट।
3. किसी अस्पताल के नेत्र विशेषज्ञ (ऑप्थेल्मोलॉजिस्ट) के पास जाएँ: सरकारी या ज़िला अस्पताल का नेत्र विभाग, मेडिकल कॉलेज, रेलवे अस्पताल, या ऐसा निजी नेत्र अस्पताल जहाँ डॉक्टर की मुहर पर योग्यता और पद लिखा हो।
4. जाँच बिना चश्मे और बिना लेंस के दें: दूर दृष्टि 6/6, 6/6 (फॉगिंग टेस्ट, +2D स्वीकार नहीं), निकट दृष्टि Sn. 0.6, 0.6, रंग दृष्टि सामान्य, द्विनेत्री/क्षेत्र/रात्रि दृष्टि सामान्य।
5. चारों कॉलम में "वास्तविक अवलोकन/मूल्य" भरा हो, कोई खाली न रहे।
6. "पूर्णतः अनुरूप हैं / अनुरूप नहीं हैं" में से एक काटा हो।
7. लैसिक सर्जरी वाली पंक्ति जैसी है वैसी रहे।
8. नेत्र विशेषज्ञ का नाम, **पंजीकरण क्रमांक**, स्थान, दिनांक, **हस्ताक्षर और मुहर** सब भरे हों; मुहर पर ऑप्थेल्मोलॉजी की शैक्षणिक योग्यता और उस अस्पताल में पद साफ़ लिखा हो (सूचना का बिंदु 5)।
9. फोटो बॉक्स में चिपका हो, नेत्र विशेषज्ञ द्वारा सत्यापित हो, नीचे आपके हस्ताक्षर हों।
10. अस्पताल से निकलने से पहले पूरा पन्ना जाँचें; दो फोटोकॉपी और फ़ोन में फोटो रखें; **मूल प्रमाणपत्र** परीक्षा के दिन साथ ले जाएँ, फोटोकॉपी स्वीकार नहीं होगी।

ऑप्टोमेट्रिस्ट, चश्मे की दुकान या बिना ऑप्थेल्मोलॉजी योग्यता वाले डॉक्टर का प्रमाणपत्र नहीं चलेगा। सूचना में सरकारी अस्पताल की शर्त नहीं है, शर्त डॉक्टर की योग्यता, पद, मुहर और पंजीकरण की है। अभी बनवा लें; एडमिट कार्ड का इंतज़ार न करें।

